UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 6, Lesson 2: Whose Country are we on', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Whose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school Aboriginal education staff or a published map. An Acknowledgement of Country can be given by anyone, while a Welcome to Country is given by Elders or Custodians of that Country.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA student guesses the Nation name from a holiday booklet. What should they do instead before using the name in an acknowledgement?\n\nFEEDBACK\nAsk local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTreating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFind out how your school or local council acknowledges Country. Record the source, the exact words and who gave you the information. Write your own respectful note for a class event once the local name is confirmed.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Whose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school Aboriginal education staff or a published map. An Acknowledgement of Country can be given by anyone, while a Welcome to Country is given by Elders or Custodians of that Country.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-ACH-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Find out how your school or local council acknowledges Country. Record the source, the exact words and who gave you the information. Write your own respectful note for a class event once the local name is confirmed. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess. Reasoning: I used Country by attending to Acknowledgement and explain why it matte rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for. Correction: Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess. Why: the correction restores accurate use of Country.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating Country on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with Country, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess. This matches the lesson skill for Country.","options":["Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess.","Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"A student guesses the Nation name from a holiday booklet. What should they do instead before using the name in an acknowledgement?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of Acknowledgement and explain why it matte with clear evidence.","Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for.","Whose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school Aboriginal education staff or a published map. An…","Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising Country?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named Acknowledgement and explain why it matte, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the Acknowledgement and explain why it matte used."],"question":"A learner is practising Country. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess.","Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about Country?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about Country."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about Acknowledgement and explain why it matte?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: Mia looks up her suburb on the AIATSIS map, writes the Nation name exactly as it is spelled there and shows it to the school''s…","Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Whose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for.","Find out how your school or local council acknowledges Country. Record the source, the exact words and who gave you the information. Write your own…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of Country?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating Country on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess. Reasoning: I used Country by attending to Acknowledgement and explain why it matte rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with Country, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for. Correction: Ask local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess. Why: the correction restores accurate use of Country.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w06-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise Country.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"First Nations Histories and Cultures","success_criteria":["I can use Country with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify Acknowledgement and explain why it matters.","I can practise Welcome independently on a new prompt.","I can notice Elders needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Whose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school Aboriginal education staff or a published map. An Acknowledgement of Country can be given by anyone, while a Welcome to Country is given by Elders or Custodians of that…","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Whose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school Aboriginal education staff or a published map. An Acknowledgement of Country can be given by anyone, while a Welcome to Country is given by Elders or Custodians of that Country.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about Country?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Mia looks up her suburb on the AIATSIS map, writes the Nation name exactly as it is spelled there and shows it to the school''s Aboriginal education officer, who confirms the wording used locally. Her acknowledgement: \"I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians of the land on which we are learning and pay respect to Elders past and present.\"\n\nTRY FIRST\nA student guesses the Nation name from a holiday booklet. What should they do instead before using the name in an acknowledgement?\n\nFEEDBACK\nAsk local sources to confirm the name and spelling, and ask how the school wants the words used. Do not guess.","explain":"Whose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school Aboriginal education staff or a published map. An Acknowledgement of Country can be given by anyone, while a Welcome to Country is given by Elders or Custodians of that Country.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Acknowledgement and Welcome compared.","caption":"Know who may give which.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Acknowledgement</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">anyone can give</text><text x=\"35