with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Treating an Acknowledgement of Country as the same as a Welcome to Country mixes up who may speak and what each is for. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about Country?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWhose Country we are on is found by asking local sources: the local Aboriginal Land Council, council, school Aboriginal education staff or a published map. An Acknowledgement of Country can be given by anyone, while a Welcome to Country is given by Elders or Custodians of that Country. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 6, Lesson 2: Whose Country are we on","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"First Nations Peoples and Country","week":6,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Mia looks up her suburb on the AIATSIS map, writes the Nation name exactly as it is spelled there and shows it to the school''s Aboriginal education officer, who confirms the wording used locally. Her acknowledgement: \"I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians of the land on which we are learning and pay respect to Elders past and present.\"\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Acknowledgement and Welcome compared.","caption":"Know who may give which.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Acknowledgement</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">anyone can give</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Welcome</text><text x=\"358\"